# Source: Slides "ICT3112 - 3 - Consequence Rule" and HoareLogic.pdf, section "Consequence rule".
id: assign-consequence-range
title: Weakening the precondition and strengthening the postcondition
topic: Skip and consequence
level: 2
source: Slides 3, Consequence Rule; HoareLogic.pdf, "Consequence rule"
tags: consequence, assignment

task:
  Prove the triple with the full consequence rule. Use the stronger
  postcondition $Q' = (10 <= y <= 90)$, calculate the weakest precondition
  $P'$ of the assignment for $Q'$, and check that it is the weaker
  precondition $1 <= x <= 9$.

pre: 2 <= x <= 8
post: 0 <= y <= 100

program:
  {P}
  {P'}
  y := x * 10     // i1
  {Q'}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Q', the stronger postcondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: Q', Q
text:
  The task gives $Q'$. The consequence rule accepts $Q'$ in place of the
  postcondition $Q$ when $Q'$ implies $Q$.
set: Q' = 10 <= y <= 90
text:
  >>> «Q'» ==> «Q»
vc: 10 <= y <= 90 ==> 0 <= y <= 100
why:
  Every number from $10$ to $90$ is between $0$ and $100$.

step: P', the weakest precondition of y := x * 10 for Q'
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  The precondition of an assignment is obtained by substituting the
  assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment expression ($x * 10$) in the
  postcondition of the statement, which is $Q'$.
calc:
  wp(y := x * 10, Q')
  = (10 <= y <= 90)[y <- x * 10]
  = 10 <= x * 10 <= 90    // y is replaced by x * 10
  = 1 <= x <= 9           // divide the three parts by 10
set: P' = 1 <= x <= 9
text:
  This is the weaker precondition $1 <= x <= 9$ given in the task.

step: P implies P'
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, P'
text:
  $P'$ is the weakest precondition of the program for $Q'$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $P'$, and $Q'$ implies $Q$. The second implication was proved in the
  first step.
  >>> «P» ==> «P'»
vc: 2 <= x <= 8 ==> 1 <= x <= 9
why:
  The interval $[2, 8]$ is included in $[1, 9]$.
instance: CONSEQUENCE
  2 <= x <= 8 ==> 1 <= x <= 9
  {1 <= x <= 9} y := x * 10 {10 <= y <= 90}
  10 <= y <= 90 ==> 0 <= y <= 100
  ---
  {2 <= x <= 8} y := x * 10 {0 <= y <= 100}
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The consequence rule proves a triple from another triple with a weaker
  precondition and a stronger postcondition. Here the triple
  $\{1 <= x <= 9\}$ `y := x * 10` $\{10 <= y <= 90\}$ is proved with the
  assignment rule, and the two implications $P ==> P'$ and $Q' ==> Q$ link
  it to the specification.
